---
title: Use Rivet in Neovim
description: Navigate project previews, run tasks, inspect errors, and control task output.
section: How-to guides
order: 2
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

[Install Rivet](/docs/start-here/#install-in-neovim) before using these commands. For a guided first project, choose a [language tutorial](/docs/start-here/#choose-your-first-project).

## Create and review

Run `:pwd` to check Neovim's working directory, then `:RivetNew`. Choose a language and either **Current folder** to generate there or **New folder** to choose a new destination. The wizard collects project choices and shows the resulting files before creation.

| In the wizard                    | Action                                                 |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| `j` / `k`                        | Move through a preview list.                           |
| Enter                            | Select an option.                                      |
| Tab                              | Move focus between the list and preview.               |
| Backspace                        | Return to the previous wizard question from a preview. |
| `:back` at a text prompt         | Return to the previous question.                       |
| `q` / Escape in a preview        | Cancel.                                                |
| `c` / Ctrl-S in the final review | Apply the displayed file plan.                         |

Inspect both paths and file contents in the final review. Existing destination files block creation, except for the reviewed `.gitignore` merge. Git initialization, if selected, reuses an existing repository or creates one without staging files or making a commit.

![Final review showing generated source files before application](/images/neovim-review.png)

Use `:RivetFile` to add a file to a project through the same review flow. See [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) for templates and target ownership, or [File comment headers](/docs/comment-headers/) for header personalization.

## Work inside a project

Open a source file beneath the project root, or change Neovim's working directory to it. Rivet discovers `.rivet.json` and supported native project markers; [Existing projects](/docs/existing-projects/) explains discovery without a Rivet manifest.

Save your edits, then run a task:

```vim
:wall
:RivetTask build
```

Choose a preset on the first task. For CMake projects, Build configures first; Run and Test configure and build first. Other providers use their native tools. Python and Lua Build check syntax. A failed or stopped step ends the workflow.

Use `:RivetTask run` to launch an application and `:RivetTask test` to run tests. Wait for each workflow to finish before starting the next.

| To change a task choice                         | Command             |
| ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |
| Configure preset or native Debug/Release choice | `:RivetPreset`      |
| Application to run                              | `:RivetRunTarget`   |
| CMake build target                              | `:RivetBuildTarget` |
| Arguments, environment, and hooks               | `:RivetRunOptions`  |
| Inspect current presets and targets             | `:RivetStatus`      |
| Repeat the last task with current choices       | `:RivetTask repeat` |

Rivet remembers task selections per project across editor restarts. See [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/) for CMake preset controls and [Runtime options](/docs/task-options/) for launch settings.

## Close and reopen task output

Output streams into a reused window. Press `q` to close the view while keeping the task running, or Enter to close completed output. `:RivetOutput` reopens the project's latest output.

Press Ctrl-C inside the output window or run `:RivetStop` to stop the local task and its process group. For remote jobs, see [Slurm cancellation](/docs/task-options/#slurm-examples).

![CTest output in the plugin task window](/images/neovim-output.png)

To close successful output automatically, include this setting in your existing setup call:

```lua
require('rivet').setup({ task_output = 'close-on-success' })
```

Failed or cancelled tasks stay visible. Retained output remains available through `:RivetOutput`.

## Follow an error

When a task fails, read its output, then run `:copen`. Rivet collects supported compiler diagnostics and runtime error locations in quickfix. Select an entry and press Enter to jump to it; use `:cnext` and `:cprevious` to move between entries.

Fix the problem, save, and run `:RivetTask repeat`. If a tool is missing or no diagnostic appears, use [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting/).

## Add a shortcut

Commands work without mappings. To add a shortcut, put this after setup in your configuration:

```lua
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>pn', '<cmd>RivetNew<cr>', {
  desc = 'Create a project',
})
```

Rivet does not install default mappings. Its commands are also subcommands of `:Rivet`, with Tab completion: for example, `:Rivet file` and `:Rivet task build`. Run `:help rivet` for the offline command reference.

## Use a local checkout

Follow [local development setup](/docs/start-here/#local-development) to load a checkout.

For other setup options, see [Configuration](/docs/configuration/). If you have an Outset installation or project, follow [Upgrading and migration](/docs/upgrading/).
